  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Polyporus radicatus, commonly known as the Rooting Polypore, is a distinctive bracket fungus characterized by a root-like mycelial base that extends into the soil. This wood-decomposing fungus is found throughout New England, typically appearing on dead hardwood logs and stumps.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Root-like Base",
      "description": "A prominent, root-like mycelial structure (rhizomorph) extends from the base of the fruiting body into the soil or wood, giving this species its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "The underside features small, angular white to cream-colored pores that are distinctly visible and typically regular in arrangement"
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Appearance",
      "description": "The upper surface is whitish to grayish with a smooth or slightly wrinkled texture, and the fruiting body is relatively thin and delicate"
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Rooting Polypore grows on dead or dying hardwood logs, stumps, and fallen branches in forests and wooded areas throughout New England, preferring moist conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The root-like structure (rhizomorph) at the base of this fungus allows it to extend nutrients and moisture from the soil into the fruiting body, an unusual adaptation among polypores that helps sustain growth on relatively dry wood.",
